From 20a733e4f5c6d7e8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 18:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvmem: cpufreq: a733: read the CPU speed bin from the eFuse
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Describe the A733 Security ID (eFuse) block to the mainline sunxi_sid
nvmem provider, expose the DVFS fuse word as an nvmem cell, and teach
sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem to turn that word into an opp-supported-hw mask and
a per-bin voltage column name. Patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` supplies the OPP table this
acts on; without `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` nothing changes, because there is no OPP table to
filter.

WHAT A SPEED BIN IS. Dies cut from one wafer are not equal. Process
variation leaves some transistors faster than others, so at the final
test stage the part is characterised and graded, and the grade is burned
into one-time fuses. Much later the kernel reads that grade back and uses
it to pick, for each frequency, the voltage this particular die needs.

It matters in both directions, and they fail differently:

  - too little voltage for the frequency: the part still runs, and
    mostly runs correctly. Setup timing fails only at the slowest
    corners of temperature, load and data pattern, so what a user sees
    is a wrong result now and then, a corrupted buffer, a crash under
    load that never reproduces on the bench. It looks exactly like a
    software bug, and it is not one.

  - too much voltage: correct, but wasteful. Dynamic power goes with
    V^2, so a 50 mV excess at 1 V is roughly 10 % of the CPU's dynamic
    power thrown away as heat, which on a passively cooled board buys a
    lower thermal trip point and therefore less sustained performance.
    The safe direction is not the free direction.

That is why we read the fuse instead of shipping one table for every
part, and it is also why the fallback below has to be worst-case rather
than average.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE SID: WHAT THE CONFIG HAD TO BE, AND WHERE THAT COMES FROM
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Block base 0x03006000, size 4K: A733 UM memory map, "SID
0x03006000---0x03006FFF  4K".

The mainline provider needs three things: where inside the block the
fuse array appears (value_offset), how much of it to expose (size), and
whether the array can be read as plain MMIO or needs the H3-style
PRCTL/RDKEY command sequence.

Answered from silicon, by dumping the whole 4K block on two dies on
2026-07-22 (the 6 GiB board and a second 6 GiB board, raw MMIO readback, not sysfs):

  0x03006000..0x030061ff  control/status. Sparse and BYTE-IDENTICAL on
                          both dies (only 0x090 = 0x3207d078,
                          0x09c = 0x1, 0x0a8 = 0x1, 0x0f0 = 0x00020001
                          are non-zero).
  0x03006200..0x0300628f  fuse content. Partly constant across dies
                          (0x200 = 0x32c05100, 0x204 = 0x4c004900,
                          0x208 = 0x58a3c418 on both) and partly per-die
                          (0x20c, 0x218..0x233, 0x238, 0x244, 0x248,
                          0x24c, 0x260, 0x264 all differ).
  0x03006290..0x03006fff  zero on both dies.

Constants where a SoC-wide identity belongs, differences where per-die
data belongs, in a window that starts exactly at +0x200: that is the
fuse array, and a plain readl of it returns it. So value_offset = 0x200
and no register readout. The H3 flag exists for an H3 erratum; there is
no sign of it here, and the values are stable (0x0300624c re-read five
times on the 6 GiB board, identical each time).

Size 0x100 covers 0x03006200..0x030062ff. Everything any of our dies has
content in lies below 0x290, and nothing above it is non-zero on either
die. We take the smallest window that provably contains data rather than
the largest that might: an oversized nvmem device answers reads with
zeroes that are indistinguishable from real fuse content, while an
undersized one fails the cell registration loudly. Fail loud, never
fabricate.

That combination - value_offset 0x200, size 0x100, direct read - is
already in mainline as sun50i_a64_cfg, and the binding already has the
idiom for reusing it: sun50i-a100-sid, sun50i-h616-sid and sun55i-a523-
sid are all documented as `<soc>-sid` plus the `allwinner,sun50i-a64-
sid` fallback, and sunxi_sid.c matches on the fallback. So the correct
patch is a compatible string, not a fourth copy of the same config
struct. sunxi_sid.c is not touched at all. Note in passing that the
A523's SID sits at the same 0x03006000 with the same size - the two SoCs
are close relatives, which is a hint, not the evidence; the evidence is
the dump above.

No clocks and no resets on the node: the block answers at boot on both
dies, under two different kernels, with nothing having enabled it.

WHAT THE UM DOES **NOT** SETTLE. Table 7-1 gives the THS calibration a
"Base Address: 0x14" inside the SID. Mainline's H616 DT places its
ths_calibration cell at nvmem offset 0x14, which is consistent with the
UM numbering being array-relative (i.e. ARM base + 0x200 + 0x14). On our
dies ARM 0x03006214 is zero on both, while a per-die block of 16-bit
values runs 0x03006218..0x03006233 (the 6 GiB board: 0x10a2, 0x1086, ... , 0x1048,
0x001c; a second 6 GiB board: 0x112c, 0x115b, ... , 0x1130, 0x0028). So the THS block is
in this array, as the UM says, but its exact layout is NOT established
and no ths_calibration cell is added here. That job and its open question
are left to the thermal patches; this patch only proves the array it lives in.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE FUSE WORD: WHAT SILICON SAYS
--------------------------------------------------------------------

DVFS word at ARM 0x0300624c = fuse offset 0x4c, bits [15:8] = speed bin.
Register readback on three chips (2026-07-22):

  the 8 GiB board  0x00000598  ->  bin 0x05
  the 6 GiB board  0x00000496  ->  bin 0x04
  a second 6 GiB board   0x00000596  ->  bin 0x05

Where a vendor kernel exports /sys/class/cpufreq/dvfs_code it agrees,
but that is a note, not the source: on a second 6 GiB board the file does not exist at
all, which is precisely why the register is the only universal source.
Bits [23:16], the second copy, are zero on all three.

The vendor's DT mapping table ("V0.9": 0x00->0x0000, 0x01->0x0100,
0x11->0x0101, 0x02->0x0200, 0x12->0x0201, 0x03->0x0300, 0x04->0x0400,
0x05->0x0500, 0x06->0x0600, 0x14->0x0401, 0x15->0x0501) is a HINT and is
treated as one. It is not encoded in this patch in any form. All it tells
us is the value range to leave room for, and BIT(0x15) = BIT(21) fits a
u32 mask with room to spare.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE CONTRACT WITH `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` - STATE IT ONCE, HERE
--------------------------------------------------------------------

BITMASK CONVENTION: one bit per bin, and the bit position IS the raw
fuse value.

  bin 0x04  ->  opp-supported-hw bit 4  (0x00000010)
  bin 0x05  ->  opp-supported-hw bit 5  (0x00000020)
  bin 0x15  ->  opp-supported-hw bit 21 (0x00200000)

An OPP that both of our known bins may use therefore carries
opp-supported-hw = <0x30>. There is no translation step, so a DT mask can
be checked against a fuse dump by eye, and adding a bin later is a DT
change with no kernel change.

VOLTAGE COLUMNS: dev_pm_opp_set_config() is called with prop_name
"speed<N>", N the same number, so the per-bin voltage property is
opp-microvolt-speed4 / opp-microvolt-speed5. drivers/opp/of.c
_parse_named_prop() falls back to the unnamed opp-microvolt when the
named one is absent, and that fallback is by design: an OPP that forgets
a bin's column gets the worst-case voltage, never a wrong one. `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`
should therefore carry a plain opp-microvolt on every OPP.

INDEX 0 IS THE WORST-CASE COLUMN. It is what an unknown die gets (see
below), so `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` must set bit 0 on every OPP that is safe on any A733,
and give those OPPs voltages that are the per-frequency maximum over all
bins. Bit 0 doubles as "raw fuse 0", which is exactly right: an all-zero
fuse word cannot be told apart from an unprogrammed one.

BOTH CLUSTERS MUST DECLARE THE SAME BIN SET. supported_hw and prop_name
are set once for every CPU, so a bin present in only one cluster's table
would leave the other cluster's policy with no OPPs at all. The driver
side of that is handled here - sunxi_cpufreq_dt_speed_mask() ANDs the
per-CPU masks, so a half-described bin demotes to worst case instead of
killing a policy - but `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` should simply keep the two tables in step.

The nvmem cell `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` must point at is labelled `cpu_speed_grade` and is
added by this patch, so `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` needs
`nvmem-cells = <&cpu_speed_grade>;` and
`compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-operating-points";` on the OPP
table node.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
4. WHY EXTEND sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem RATHER THAN WRITE SOMETHING NEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------

A new driver would have to do what this one already does - find the CPU
OPP descriptor node, match its compatible, read one nvmem cell, call
dev_pm_opp_set_config() with supported_hw and prop_name for every
present CPU, then register cpufreq-dt - and would duplicate all of it to
change one field extraction. The existing driver's structure (a per-SoC
efuse_xlate behind an of_device_id) exists exactly for this. The A733
addition is one xlate, one data struct, one OPP-table compatible and one
machine compatible.

The one shared helper added, sunxi_cpufreq_dt_speed_mask(), is new code
that only the A733 xlate calls. dt_has_supported_hw() is deliberately
left untouched even though the new helper could subsume it, because that
would change probe behaviour for H6/A100/H616 - SoCs we have no hardware
for and therefore no right to change.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
5. THE FALLBACK, AND HOW THIS DIFFERS FROM THE VENDOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The vendor kernel does not filter OPPs on purpose. Its per-bin tables
carry a 0 voltage for the steps a bin does not support, and those entries
disappear later because _opp_supported_by_regulators() rejects them. That
is a side effect of the regulator check, and it has two consequences we
do not want: it only works once cpu-supply is wired, and where it does
not work, nothing complains. A second 6 GiB board demonstrates the failure mode -
it lists the full union of the vendor table and was caught by the PLL
register actually running 1300 MHz (N=50) at 800 mV, a point its own bin
has no voltage entry for. We do the filtering deliberately and up front,
with opp-supported-hw, so a mis-described bin produces an empty table
and a failed probe rather than an undervolted core.

The fallback to index 0 is taken, with a log line every time, when:

  - bits [15:8] read 0. Unprogrammed and "graded 0" are the same
    observation from outside. The vendor has a table for the zero case;
    we refuse to guess which of the two we are looking at.
  - the bin does not fit a u32 bit position (>= 32).
  - no OPP in DT claims that bin, on every CPU. A die whose voltages we
    have never measured must not inherit a table measured on a better
    die.
  - bits [23:16], the second copy, are programmed. The vendor prefers
    that copy over the primary when it is non-zero. On all three chips
    we can read it is zero, so the preference is untestable, and acting
    on an untestable hint here means possibly selecting a FASTER table
    than the primary fuse says. We treat such a part as unknown instead.
    This is the one place where we are knowingly more conservative than
    the vendor, and it is the first thing to revisit if a die ever shows
    up with those bits set - the log line names the value.

Every one of these is safe by construction only because index 0 is
defined as the worst case. That definition is `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`'s to honour.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
6. WHAT THIS BUYS, AND THE LIMIT THAT IS NOT NEGOTIABLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The alternative already running in this project's earlier Armbian-based
port of this board is a single bin-neutral table whose voltage at each
frequency is the maximum over all bins. It is correct and it is what index 0 will be. Its price,
measured rather than assumed: on a bin-0x05 part it asks for up to 50 mV
more than the die needs between 1612 and 2002 MHz. Reading the fuse gives
that back on every part that is not worst case, which is at least two of
our three boards.

HARD LIMIT, ABSOLUTE: no OPP may exceed what the datasheet permits for
VDD-CPUB/VDD-CPUL, which is 1.2 V absolute maximum. The highest voltage
seen across all nine vendor bins is 1050 mV, and nothing in this series
may go above it without a documented reason. This patch sets no voltage
at all - it selects which column `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`'s voltages are read from - but the
limit is stated here because it is the constraint the whole mechanism
exists to respect.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
7. VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED / PENDING
--------------------------------------------------------------------

VERIFIED ON SILICON (2026-07-22, raw MMIO readback):
  - SID block responds at 0x03006000 with no clock or reset set up.
  - fuse array window at +0x200: layout argument above, two full 4K
    dumps (the 6 GiB board, a second 6 GiB board).
  - DVFS word 0x0300624c: 0x00000496 (the 6 GiB board), 0x00000596 (a second 6 GiB board),
    0x00000598 (the 8 GiB board); bits [23:16] zero on all three; value stable
    across repeated reads.

NOT VERIFIED: applies-clean and builds are separate claims and are the
reviewer's to make. The THS calibration layout is explicitly NOT claimed.

THE ONE OBSERVABLE THAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS. With `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` + `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` applied,
boot the SAME Image and the SAME dtb on the 6 GiB board (bin 4) and the 8 GiB board (bin 5),
drive the A76 cluster to 1898 MHz, confirm the operating point from
CPU_B_PLL_CTRL (N=73, EXT_DIVP = /1) rather than from sysfs, then read
VDD-CPUB from the PMIC - AXP318W at i2c 0x36, DCDC3 voltage register
0x14, bits [6:0] = (mV - 500)/10:

    the 6 GiB board must read 0x2f (970 mV)   the 8 GiB board must read 0x2c (940 mV)

Two dies, one binary, two different voltages, in the direction the fuse
words say. If both boards land on the same value, or if either boot log
contains "using the worst-case OPP column", this patch has not done its
job. (The two numbers themselves are `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`'s measured columns; what `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse`
is on the hook for is that the boards diverge at all.)

AND THE FALLBACK PATH, TESTABLE WITHOUT TOUCHING A FUSE. Build a test
dtb with the cell moved to `cpu-speed-grade@40 { reg = <0x40 4>; }`.
ARM 0x03006240 reads 0x00000000 on both the 6 GiB board and a second 6 GiB board (dumped
2026-07-22), so the driver must log "no usable speed bin in fuse word
0x00000000", select index 0, and the board must still boot and scale on
the worst-case column. Read-only, no fuse write, exercises the whole
unknown-die path.

KCONFIG. CONFIG_NVMEM_SUNXI_SID is already =y in the arm64 defconfig and
CONFIG_ARM_ALLWINNER_SUN50I_CPUFREQ_NVMEM is =m. Both are set built-in
(=y) in the recipe's kernel/6.18/config, so that the OPP configuration is
applied before userspace can command a frequency; that is a repo-side
setting and not part of this patch file.

DEPENDS. Foundation only for the DT node; the cpufreq half is inert
until `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` lands. The sid node is inserted between clock-controller@
2002000 and interrupt-controller@3400000, chosen to sit away from the
regions `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` to `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` (DCXO / oscillator model / CPU-PLL) are editing.

